# the relation of the inner loop compares its counter with the counter of the outer loop, so the edge that leaves the inner loop narrows the outer counter in all that stands behind it, the step of the outer counter too.
# In look 1 at the inner loop nothing is left of either operand on that edge: its counter is 0 and no turn has been seen. A look at the outer loop comes to that edge once in each look at the inner one, and the last one counts
# function run(n) { let s = 0; for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) s += j; return s; }
